{
  "dom_id": "question-169106",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "The moment of inertia of the plate about the axis perpendicular to the plate passing through its center of mass is ${I}_{M}=\\frac{78}{5}.$ Find the moment of inertia ${I}_{l}$ of the plate about the axis $l$ that is perpendicular to the plate and passes through the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${I}_{l}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nIn part 1, we found that the mass of the plate is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M=24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe squared distance between the line through the center of mass $(2,\\frac{3}{2})$ and the line through the point $(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}^{2} & ={(2-1)}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2}-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, applying the parallel axis theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & =\\frac{78}{5}+\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot 24 \\\\  & =\\frac{78}{5}+30 \\\\  & =\\frac{228}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
